#Lessons Learned:

#Humans are typically the most vulnerable targets for attackers to exploit. While executing code and using hacking tools can get one into the targeted system, it often takes a 
#lot of time

#Humans, however, are much easier to manipulate. one click, or even one secret shared, can be significantly more impactful than any line of code

#Attackers often execute attacks based on manipulating human psychology, known as "social engineering." These attacks are reliant on two factors: Trust and Emotions

#Attackers will do everything they can to make themselves appear trustworthy - similar email addresses to real companies, pretending to have shared values, etc.

#Attackers also rely heavily on emotions. Senses of urgency, fear, or curiousity can be heavily manipulated

#Defending humans is vital in defending systems. Catching an attack before it ever reaches a human is ideal. Since it's not always possible, teaching human users to report suspected
#attacks is necessary. SOC teams handle everything that got through both their tools and the training given to their protected humans.

#Humans are vulnerable - no one is perfect. Even with training, we are all prone to error. As SOC analysts, it's our duty to help prevent data loss and damage to systems when access
#is improperly given. It's better to verify and delay a process/approval than it is to grant access to the wrong person!

